.Dd October 30, 2026
.Dt COVACATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm covacate
.Nd leave the calling thread vacant until its code comes back
.Sh LIBRARY
.Lb libc
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ft int
.Fn covacate void
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Fn covacate
system call blocks the calling thread until the thread's own code comes
back to it.
Until then, the thread is vacant.
It is part of the colocated call mechanism of
.Xr cocall 2
and
.Xr coaccept 2 ,
and programs do not call it directly.
.Pp
A cocall runs the callee's code on the caller's thread.
When
.Xr coaccept 2
has no caller to reply to, as on its first call, it saves the callee's
registers for the next cocall to restore, and then calls
.Fn covacate .
The callee's thread stays in
.Fn covacate
while the callee's code runs on callers' threads.
.Pp
Suppose that process A cocalls process B, and that B's code
then makes a system call on A's thread.
The system call must run on B's thread, as B's process.
The kernel swaps the saved user registers of the two threads, so that B's
thread holds B's system call and A's thread holds B's call to
.Fn covacate .
The kernel then wakes B's thread, which makes the system call.
A's thread is now in
.Fn covacate ,
and stays vacant until A's code comes back to it.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
When the thread's code comes back,
.Fn covacate
does not return.
Instead, the thread runs that code from where it stopped on the other thread.
In the example above, B's thread makes the system call that B's code made on
A's thread.
If a signal interrupts the wait,
.Fn covacate
returns -1 and sets the global variable
.Va errno
to indicate the error.
.Sh ERRORS
The
.Fn covacate
system call will fail if:
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the wait.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr coaccept 2 ,
.Xr cocall 2
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Fn covacate
system call first appeared in CheriBSD, named
.Fn copark .
